.Dd February 19, 2026
.Dt ELF_VALIDATE 3
.Os Substrate
.Sh NAME
.Nm elf_validate
.Nd ELF object validation and diagnostics
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In elfobj.h
.Ft elf_err_t
.Fo elf_validate
.Fa "elfobj_t *obj"
.Fa "char **diagnostics"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Fn elf_validate
function performs structural validation of an ELF object, checking for
format errors, inconsistencies, and potential security issues.
.Pp
Validation is essential when:
.Bl -bullet
.It
Processing untrusted ELF files from external sources.
.It
Ensuring generated ELF files are well-formed.
.It
Debugging linker or loader issues.
.It
Implementing security-sensitive tools (debuggers, profilers, etc.).
.El
.Ss Validation Checks
The validation process performs the following checks:
.Bl -tag -width "Section Overlap"
.It Sy ELF Header Validation
.Bl -bullet
.It
Magic number verification (0x7F 'E' 'L' 'F').
.It
Class validation (32-bit or 64-bit).
.It
Data encoding validation (little-endian or big-endian).
.It
Version check.
.It
Type field validation.
.It
Machine type validation.
.El
.It Sy Section Header Validation
.Bl -bullet
.It
Section count and offset bounds checking.
.It
Section name string table validation.
.It
Section type field validation.
.It
Section offset and size bounds checking.
.El
.It Sy Section Overlap Detection
.Bl -bullet
.It
Detection of overlapping section regions.
.It
Special handling for
.Dv SHT_NOBITS
sections (which do not occupy file space).
.El
.It Sy Symbol Table Validation
.Bl -bullet
.It
Symbol entry size and count validation.
.It
Symbol name string table bounds checking.
.It
Symbol binding and type field validation.
.It
Section index validation.
.El
.It Sy Relocation Validation
.Bl -bullet
.It
Relocation entry size and count validation.
.It
Relocation offset bounds checking.
.It
Relocation symbol index validation.
.It
Relocation type validation (architecture-specific).
.El
.El
.Ss Security Guarantees
The validation function provides the following security guarantees:
.Bl -tag -width "Memory Safety"
.It Sy Memory Safety
The function will not read or write outside the bounds of the ELF
image buffer.
All offsets and sizes are validated before use.
.It Sy Integer Overflow Protection
All arithmetic operations on offsets and sizes are checked for overflow.
.It Sy NULL Pointer Safety
The function handles NULL pointers gracefully and returns an appropriate
error code.
.It Sy No Side Effects
Validation does not modify the ELF object or its underlying data.
.El
.Pp
These guarantees make
.Fn elf_validate
suitable for processing untrusted input, such as:
.Bl -bullet
.It
User-uploaded ELF files.
.It
Downloaded packages or plugins.
.It
Network-received binaries.
.El
.Ss Diagnostic Output
When validation fails, the
.Fa diagnostics
parameter can be used to retrieve a human-readable description of
the problems found.
.Pp
If
.Fa diagnostics
is non-NULL and validation fails, a newly allocated string is stored
in
.Fa *diagnostics .
This string contains one or more diagnostic messages, each on a
separate line.
The caller is responsible for freeing this string with
.Xr free 3 .
.Pp
If validation succeeds, an empty string is stored in
.Fa *diagnostics
(if non-NULL).
.Ss Fuzzing Considerations
The
.Nm libelfobj
library is designed to be robust against malformed input and is
suitable for use in fuzzing campaigns.
.Pp
When fuzzing ELF parsers:
.Bl -bullet
.It
Use
.Fn elf_open_memory
with fuzz-generated buffers.
.It
Call
.Fn elf_validate
to check for structural issues.
.It
Handle all error codes appropriately.
.It
Use memory sanitizers (AddressSanitizer, MemorySanitizer) to detect
memory safety issues.
.El
.Pp
The library has been tested with:
.Bl -bullet
.It
AddressSanitizer (ASan) for memory errors.
.It
UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer (UBSan) for undefined behavior.
.It
Coverage-guided fuzzing with libFuzzer.
.El
.Ss Diagnostic API
Diagnostic messages are accumulated during validation and can be
retrieved via the
.Fa diagnostics
parameter.
Each message describes a specific validation failure.
.Pp
Message format:
.Bd -literal
<description> [context]
.Ed
.Pp
Examples:
.Bd -literal
section out of file bounds index=5
section overlap index=3
section overlap peer=7
relocation offset out of range idx=12
relocation has NULL section
.Ed
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn elf_validate
returns
.Dv ELF_OK
if the object passes all validation checks.
.Pp
If validation fails,
.Dv ELF_ERR_FORMAT
is returned and diagnostic messages are available via the
.Fa diagnostics
parameter.
.Pp
.Dv ELF_ERR_STATE
is returned if
.Fa obj
is NULL.
.Pp
.Dv ELF_ERR_OOM
is returned if memory allocation for diagnostics fails.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Ss Basic Validation
.Bd -literal
#include <elfobj.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    elfobj_t *obj;
    elf_err_t err;
    char *diag;

    if (argc < 2) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Usage: %s <elf-file>\\n", argv[0]);
        return 1;
    }

    err = elf_open(argv[1], &obj);
    if (err != ELF_OK) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Cannot open %s: %s\\n", argv[1], elf_errstr(err));
        return 1;
    }

    err = elf_validate(obj, &diag);
    if (err != ELF_OK) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Validation failed:\\n%s\\n", diag);
        free(diag);
        elf_close(obj);
        return 1;
    }

    printf("Validation passed\\n");
    free(diag);
    elf_close(obj);
    return 0;
}
.Ed
.Ss Validation Without Diagnostics
.Bd -literal
#include <elfobj.h>

int validate_elf(const char *path) {
    elfobj_t *obj;
    elf_err_t err;

    err = elf_open(path, &obj);
    if (err != ELF_OK) {
        return -1;  /* Cannot open */
    }

    /* Validate without retrieving diagnostics */
    err = elf_validate(obj, NULL);
    elf_close(obj);

    return (err == ELF_OK) ? 0 : -1;
}
.Ed
.Ss Processing Untrusted Input
.Bd -literal
#include <elfobj.h>
#include <stdio.h>

/*
 * Safely process an ELF file from an untrusted source.
 * Returns 0 on success, -1 on error.
 */
int process_untrusted_elf(const void *data, size_t size) {
    elfobj_t *obj = NULL;
    elf_err_t err;
    char *diag = NULL;
    int result = -1;

    /* Parse the ELF image */
    err = elf_open_memory(data, size, &obj);
    if (err != ELF_OK) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Parse error: %s\\n", elf_errstr(err));
        goto cleanup;
    }

    /* Validate the structure */
    err = elf_validate(obj, &diag);
    if (err != ELF_OK) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Validation error:\\n%s\\n", diag);
        goto cleanup;
    }

    /* Object is valid; safe to process */
    /* ... application-specific processing ... */

    result = 0;

cleanup:
    if (diag) free(diag);
    if (obj) elf_close(obj);
    return result;
}
.Ed
.Ss Integration with Fuzzing Harness
.Bd -literal
#include <elfobj.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

/*
 * Fuzzing harness for libFuzzer.
 * Compile with:
 *   clang -fsanitize=address -fuzzer-one-callsome \
 *         elf_fuzz.c -lelfobj -o elf_fuzz
 */
int LLVMFuzzerTestOneInput(const uint8_t *data, size_t size) {
    elfobj_t *obj = NULL;
    elf_err_t err;

    /* Minimum size for ELF header */
    if (size < 16) {
        return 0;
    }

    /* Try to parse the input */
    err = elf_open_memory(data, size, &obj);
    if (err != ELF_OK) {
        /* Expected for malformed input */
        return 0;
    }

    /* Validate the structure */
    err = elf_validate(obj, NULL);
    (void)err;  /* Result doesn't matter for fuzzing */

    /* Clean up */
    elf_close(obj);
    return 0;
}
.Ed
.Ss Detailed Diagnostic Processing
.Bd -literal
#include <elfobj.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    elfobj_t *obj;
    elf_err_t err;
    char *diag;
    char *line;

    if (argc < 2) return 1;

    err = elf_open(argv[1], &obj);
    if (err != ELF_OK) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Open failed: %s\\n", elf_errstr(err));
        return 1;
    }

    err = elf_validate(obj, &diag);
    if (err != ELF_OK) {
        printf("Validation issues in %s:\\n", argv[1]);

        /* Parse each diagnostic line */
        line = strtok(diag, "\\n");
        while (line != NULL) {
            printf("  - %s\\n", line);
            line = strtok(NULL, "\\n");
        }

        free(diag);
        elf_close(obj);
        return 1;
    }

    printf("No validation issues\\n");
    free(diag);
    elf_close(obj);
    return 0;
}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr elfobj 3 ,
.Xr elf 5
.Sh STANDARDS
Validation follows the ELF specification constraints.
See
.Xr elfobj 3
for references.
.Sh HISTORY
The validation function was added to
.Nm libelfobj
to support secure processing of untrusted ELF files in toolchain
utilities and the dynamic linker.
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
.Bl -bullet
.It
Always validate ELF files from untrusted sources before processing.
.It
Validation is necessary but not sufficient for security; application
code must also handle error conditions properly.
.It
The library does not check for semantic correctness (e.g., whether
relocations are valid for the target architecture).
.It
Resource exhaustion attacks (via extremely large section counts or
sizes) are mitigated by bounds checking but may still cause memory
allocation failures.
.El
.Sh BUGS
.Bl -bullet
.It
Architecture-specific validation (e.g., relocation type checking)
is not fully implemented.
.It
Program header validation is not implemented.
.It
Dynamic section validation is not implemented.
.El
